1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:02,980
There is a technique taught for decades to

2
00:00:03,079 --> 00:00:05,960
police interrogators across the United States

3
00:00:06,460 --> 00:00:09,179
that can make an innocent person confess to

4
00:00:09,239 --> 00:00:12,199
a crime they did not commit in under

5
00:00:12,239 --> 00:00:15,380
an hour without a single hand ever being

6
00:00:15,479 --> 00:00:18,180
laid on them. It doesn't work by force.

7
00:00:18,739 --> 00:00:21,779
It works by psychology, and once you understand

8
00:00:21,819 --> 00:00:24,719
the structure underneath it, you'll start noticing it

9
00:00:24,819 --> 00:00:27,680
everywhere long after the interrogation room.

10
00:00:28,299 --> 00:00:30,655
This is Shadow Figure. For much of the

11
00:00:30,715 --> 00:00:33,824
last seventy years, one method has dominated

12
00:00:33,955 --> 00:00:37,395
police interrogation training in North America: the

13
00:00:37,475 --> 00:00:40,915
Reid technique. It isn't one trick. It's a

14
00:00:40,975 --> 00:00:44,315
structure built in stages, and each stage

15
00:00:44,475 --> 00:00:47,515
exists to make the next one easier. The

16
00:00:47,555 --> 00:00:50,715
first stage is presumption. The interrogator

17
00:00:50,725 --> 00:00:53,795
doesn't walk in asking whether you did it.

18
00:00:53,805 --> 00:00:56,775
They walk in already certain that you did,

19
00:00:56,795 --> 00:00:59,225
and the entire conversation is built on that

20
00:00:59,255 --> 00:01:02,275
certainty. You are rarely asked, "Did you do

21
00:01:02,375 --> 00:01:05,395
this?" You are told directly or through

22
00:01:05,455 --> 00:01:08,804
implication that the evidence already exists,

23
00:01:09,315 --> 00:01:12,365
that this conversation is about what happens next,

24
00:01:12,855 --> 00:01:15,595
not about whether it happened at all. The

25
00:01:15,655 --> 00:01:19,045
second stage is moral minimization. Once

26
00:01:19,135 --> 00:01:22,555
presumption has done its work, the interrogator stops

27
00:01:22,655 --> 00:01:25,715
treating the act as monstrous and starts treating

28
00:01:25,775 --> 00:01:29,275
it as understandable. Anyone in your situation

29
00:01:29,715 --> 00:01:32,235
might have done the same thing. This doesn't

30
00:01:32,295 --> 00:01:35,255
make you a bad person. The specific words

31
00:01:35,315 --> 00:01:38,555
change, but the function doesn't. Lower the

32
00:01:38,575 --> 00:01:41,715
psychological cost of admitting it. A confession

33
00:01:41,855 --> 00:01:45,035
feels far less catastrophic when the person asking

34
00:01:45,095 --> 00:01:48,055
for it has just spent ten minutes explaining

35
00:01:48,115 --> 00:01:51,135
why it was almost reasonable. The third stage

36
00:01:51,495 --> 00:01:53,995
is the false alternative, and this is the

37
00:01:54,035 --> 00:01:57,315
part most people never notice because it's disguised

38
00:01:57,375 --> 00:02:00,115
as a choice. You are not asked, "Did

39
00:02:00,135 --> 00:02:02,815
you do this?" You are asked something like,

40
00:02:03,175 --> 00:02:05,375
"Did you plan this, or did it just

41
00:02:05,455 --> 00:02:08,525
happen in the moment?" Notice what both branches

42
00:02:08,575 --> 00:02:11,335
of that question have in common. Neither one

43
00:02:11,355 --> 00:02:13,995
lets you say no. The only real decision

44
00:02:14,055 --> 00:02:16,155
left on the table is which flavor of

45
00:02:16,235 --> 00:02:19,455
guilty you'd prefer to be. Stacked together:

46
00:02:20,015 --> 00:02:22,995
certainty, minimization, false choice.

47
00:02:23,535 --> 00:02:26,935
The structure doesn't just make confession easier. Under

48
00:02:26,995 --> 00:02:30,325
enough time, fatigue, and psychological pressure,

49
00:02:30,775 --> 00:02:32,935
it can make confession feel like the only

50
00:02:32,975 --> 00:02:35,415
way out of the room. None of this

51
00:02:35,455 --> 00:02:38,615
requires shouting, threats, or physical force.

52
00:02:39,135 --> 00:02:42,295
In fact, the technique is built specifically to

53
00:02:42,335 --> 00:02:45,275
work without any of that because a calm,

54
00:02:45,595 --> 00:02:48,895
patient, endlessly certain interrogator is

55
00:02:48,955 --> 00:02:51,555
far harder to resist than an aggressive one.

56
00:02:52,215 --> 00:02:54,815
Aggression gives you something to push back against.

57
00:02:55,475 --> 00:02:58,595
Calm, total certainty gives you nothing to argue

58
00:02:58,615 --> 00:03:02,055
with because the conversation was never framed as

59
00:03:02,125 --> 00:03:04,963
a debate about whether it happened. It was

60
00:03:05,083 --> 00:03:07,982
only ever framed as a conversation about what

61
00:03:08,043 --> 00:03:11,303
happens next. Time itself becomes part of the

62
00:03:11,402 --> 00:03:14,543
mechanism. Interrogations built on this structure

63
00:03:14,803 --> 00:03:17,803
can run for hours, sometimes many hours,

64
00:03:18,362 --> 00:03:20,782
long past the point where a tired, frightened

65
00:03:20,842 --> 00:03:24,322
person is thinking clearly about consequences beyond

66
00:03:24,362 --> 00:03:27,682
the next five minutes. The goal, whether stated

67
00:03:27,743 --> 00:03:30,662
or not, is to keep narrowing the psychological

68
00:03:30,782 --> 00:03:33,682
room in that chair until the only exit

69
00:03:33,782 --> 00:03:36,102
anyone can still see is the one the

70
00:03:36,162 --> 00:03:39,403
interrogator has been pointing to the entire time.

71
00:03:39,903 --> 00:03:42,482
Picture how the three stages actually stack in

72
00:03:42,543 --> 00:03:45,522
a real room. The first hour is presumption,

73
00:03:45,842 --> 00:03:49,102
repeated calmly without ever really engaging with a

74
00:03:49,162 --> 00:03:52,162
denial. The denial isn't argued against.

75
00:03:52,602 --> 00:03:56,102
It's simply set aside over and over

76
00:03:56,182 --> 00:03:58,342
as if it were an interruption rather than

77
00:03:58,402 --> 00:04:00,942
an answer. By the second hour,

78
00:04:01,222 --> 00:04:04,722
minimization arrives. A softer tone, an offer

79
00:04:04,762 --> 00:04:07,762
to understand, a suggestion that this was a

80
00:04:07,822 --> 00:04:10,882
mistake rather than a crime, that people make

81
00:04:10,942 --> 00:04:14,062
mistakes, that this can be worked through. By

82
00:04:14,122 --> 00:04:16,902
then, denial has stopped feeling like it's even

83
00:04:16,962 --> 00:04:19,582
being heard, and the exhausted person in the

84
00:04:19,662 --> 00:04:22,702
chair starts listening less for whether the accusation

85
00:04:22,743 --> 00:04:25,222
is true and more for whatever will make

86
00:04:25,262 --> 00:04:28,502
the room stop closing in. The false alternative

87
00:04:28,622 --> 00:04:31,522
arrives last, almost like relief.

88
00:04:31,603 --> 00:04:34,182
Finally, a question that sounds like it has

89
00:04:34,203 --> 00:04:37,682
an answer. It's rarely a coincidence that it

90
00:04:37,782 --> 00:04:40,802
arrives only once the first two stages have

91
00:04:40,842 --> 00:04:44,288
already done their work. Here is the uncomfortable

92
00:04:44,428 --> 00:04:47,828
part. This same structure applied to someone

93
00:04:47,868 --> 00:04:50,778
who is actually guilty can produce a truthful

94
00:04:50,868 --> 00:04:54,128
confession. Applied to someone who is innocent,

95
00:04:54,728 --> 00:04:58,148
someone exhausted, frightened, young, or

96
00:04:58,208 --> 00:05:01,448
simply worn down by hours of unwavering certainty

97
00:05:01,528 --> 00:05:04,128
aimed at them, it can produce a false

98
00:05:04,188 --> 00:05:07,648
one. Organizations that work on wrongful conviction

99
00:05:07,708 --> 00:05:10,718
cases, including the Innocence Project, have

100
00:05:10,748 --> 00:05:13,708
documented real cases where DNA evidence

101
00:05:13,888 --> 00:05:16,808
uncovered years later proved the innocence of

102
00:05:16,908 --> 00:05:20,108
people who had confessed under exactly this kind

103
00:05:20,148 --> 00:05:22,708
of pressure. Not people who were lying about

104
00:05:22,748 --> 00:05:25,968
being pressured. People who, in the moment, could

105
00:05:25,978 --> 00:05:27,608
no longer see a path out of the

106
00:05:27,668 --> 00:05:30,108
room that didn't run through saying the words

107
00:05:30,328 --> 00:05:33,328
the interrogator needed to hear. That should sound

108
00:05:33,368 --> 00:05:36,168
impossible to you. How does an innocent person

109
00:05:36,268 --> 00:05:39,348
confess to something they didn't do? But sit

110
00:05:39,428 --> 00:05:42,488
with the structure again. You are told the

111
00:05:42,568 --> 00:05:45,888
evidence already proves you did it, so denial

112
00:05:46,008 --> 00:05:48,908
stops feeling like an option that's actually being

113
00:05:48,988 --> 00:05:51,968
heard. You are told it wouldn't make you

114
00:05:52,028 --> 00:05:55,268
a bad person, so admitting it stops feeling

115
00:05:55,288 --> 00:05:57,448
like the end of the world. And the

116
00:05:57,548 --> 00:06:00,588
only question left on the table already assumes

117
00:06:00,628 --> 00:06:03,888
you did it. Under those conditions, for long

118
00:06:03,968 --> 00:06:06,428
enough, just tell them what they want can

119
00:06:06,438 --> 00:06:08,248
start to feel like the only way to

120
00:06:08,288 --> 00:06:10,928
make the pressure stop, even when what they

121
00:06:10,948 --> 00:06:13,798
want is a lie. This isn't a condemnation

122
00:06:13,848 --> 00:06:16,278
of every interrogator who has ever used this

123
00:06:16,328 --> 00:06:18,948
method, and it isn't a claim that it

124
00:06:18,988 --> 00:06:21,858
never produces true confessions. It's a

125
00:06:21,948 --> 00:06:25,048
documented, uncomfortable fact. A technique

126
00:06:25,348 --> 00:06:28,488
built to extract truth can, under the wrong

127
00:06:28,528 --> 00:06:31,988
conditions, extract compliance instead. And

128
00:06:32,028 --> 00:06:34,708
the person saying the words often can't tell

129
00:06:34,728 --> 00:06:37,148
the difference between the two while it's happening.

130
00:06:38,188 --> 00:06:40,788
The people most vulnerable to this aren't the

131
00:06:40,848 --> 00:06:44,148
hardened, streetwise suspects the technique was

132
00:06:44,188 --> 00:06:47,088
originally imagined for. They're teenagers,

133
00:06:47,488 --> 00:06:50,948
people with intellectual or developmental disabilities,

134
00:06:51,388 --> 00:06:54,548
and people who are simply exhausted, frightened, or

135
00:06:54,708 --> 00:06:57,898
eager, almost desperately, to please the authority

136
00:06:58,008 --> 00:07:00,448
figure in front of them. Youth and

137
00:07:00,508 --> 00:07:03,498
vulnerability don't make someone more likely to have

138
00:07:03,508 --> 00:07:06,558
committed a crime. They make someone more likely

139
00:07:06,568 --> 00:07:08,988
to say they did, if saying so is

140
00:07:09,048 --> 00:07:11,428
the only thing that seems likely to end

141
00:07:11,468 --> 00:07:14,708
the pressure. There's a particular cruelty in how

142
00:07:14,748 --> 00:07:17,949
this affects people who are naturally cooperative and

143
00:07:18,029 --> 00:07:21,509
trusting of authority. The very traits society

144
00:07:21,649 --> 00:07:24,909
usually calls good character become the exact

145
00:07:24,949 --> 00:07:28,129
lever the structure exploits. Someone

146
00:07:28,209 --> 00:07:31,569
raised to respect authority, to believe honesty

147
00:07:31,629 --> 00:07:34,809
is always rewarded, to assume the person across

148
00:07:34,889 --> 00:07:38,129
the table is fundamentally fair, walks into that

149
00:07:38,209 --> 00:07:41,409
room with far fewer defenses than someone who

150
00:07:41,489 --> 00:07:44,929
has learned, often from harder experience, to

151
00:07:44,969 --> 00:07:48,369
distrust confident certainty on principle. Age

152
00:07:48,489 --> 00:07:50,949
compounds all of this in ways that are

153
00:07:51,029 --> 00:07:54,409
easy to underestimate. Adolescents' brains are,

154
00:07:54,679 --> 00:07:57,989
on average, less equipped than adults to weigh

155
00:07:58,049 --> 00:08:01,429
a distant consequence against an immediate one, which

156
00:08:01,489 --> 00:08:04,469
means the promise of this ends now

157
00:08:04,989 --> 00:08:07,669
if you just say it can outweigh even

158
00:08:07,729 --> 00:08:10,729
a serious future penalty in a way it

159
00:08:10,769 --> 00:08:13,929
wouldn't for someone older. A technique that

160
00:08:14,009 --> 00:08:17,129
already exploits fatigue and the desire to please

161
00:08:17,149 --> 00:08:20,509
authority becomes considerably more effective

162
00:08:20,589 --> 00:08:23,429
against a mind that hasn't finished developing the

163
00:08:23,469 --> 00:08:26,649
machinery to resist exactly that trade-off.

164
00:08:27,329 --> 00:08:30,169
This concern is serious enough that some

165
00:08:30,209 --> 00:08:32,789
countries have moved away from this model

166
00:08:32,829 --> 00:08:36,089
entirely. The United Kingdom and Ireland, for

167
00:08:36,149 --> 00:08:39,619
instance, shifted decades ago toward an approach

168
00:08:39,909 --> 00:08:43,269
often called the peace model, built around open

169
00:08:43,729 --> 00:08:47,229
information-gathering questions instead of presumed guilt,

170
00:08:47,729 --> 00:08:49,689
moral minimization, and false

171
00:08:49,769 --> 00:08:53,089
alternatives. The existence of that alternative

172
00:08:53,189 --> 00:08:56,149
matters because it proves this isn't the only

173
00:08:56,249 --> 00:08:59,749
way to conduct an effective interrogation. It's simply

174
00:08:59,789 --> 00:09:02,809
the version that treats extracting a confession as

175
00:09:02,869 --> 00:09:05,709
the goal rather than a byproduct of finding

176
00:09:05,789 --> 00:09:08,549
out what's actually true. Part of what makes

177
00:09:08,589 --> 00:09:11,939
false confessions so dangerous afterward is how

178
00:09:11,969 --> 00:09:14,878
convincing they look to everyone who wasn't in

179
00:09:14,889 --> 00:09:17,789
the room. A jury hearing, "I did it

180
00:09:18,069 --> 00:09:21,049
in my own words on video," rarely stops

181
00:09:21,089 --> 00:09:23,649
to ask what happened in the hours before

182
00:09:23,689 --> 00:09:27,049
that camera started rolling. A confession sounds

183
00:09:27,089 --> 00:09:30,459
like the strongest possible evidence, which is exactly

184
00:09:30,509 --> 00:09:32,929
why the pressure to produce one, even a

185
00:09:32,989 --> 00:09:36,129
false one, can feel justified to an interrogator

186
00:09:36,549 --> 00:09:39,609
convinced they already have the right person. That

187
00:09:39,649 --> 00:09:42,689
conviction, held firmly enough, stops being a

188
00:09:42,749 --> 00:09:46,109
hypothesis being tested and starts being a conclusion

189
00:09:46,469 --> 00:09:49,789
the entire conversation is built to confirm. This

190
00:09:49,849 --> 00:09:52,449
is part of why full recording of interrogations,

191
00:09:52,949 --> 00:09:55,529
now required in a growing number of jurisdictions,

192
00:09:56,049 --> 00:09:58,369
matters so much. It's one of the few

193
00:09:58,409 --> 00:10:01,789
tools that can show later exactly which

194
00:10:01,869 --> 00:10:05,322
stage produced which words. You are very unlikely

195
00:10:05,362 --> 00:10:07,912
to ever sit in a police interrogation room.

196
00:10:08,542 --> 00:10:10,931
But the exact same three-stage structure,

197
00:10:11,482 --> 00:10:14,702
certainty, minimization, false alternative,

198
00:10:15,222 --> 00:10:17,762
shows up far outside it, and that's the

199
00:10:17,802 --> 00:10:20,802
part worth carrying with you. A manager who

200
00:10:20,842 --> 00:10:23,542
says, "We already know this was your mistake.

201
00:10:24,102 --> 00:10:26,122
The only question is whether you're going to

202
00:10:26,202 --> 00:10:28,122
own up to it or make this worse,"

203
00:10:28,642 --> 00:10:31,282
is running stage one. A partner in an

204
00:10:31,362 --> 00:10:33,762
argument who says, "I get why you'd do

205
00:10:33,802 --> 00:10:37,042
that. Anyone would be tempted," before asking you

206
00:10:37,062 --> 00:10:40,212
to admit something is running stage two. And

207
00:10:40,262 --> 00:10:42,862
anyone who offers you two options where both

208
00:10:42,902 --> 00:10:45,682
quietly assume the same conclusion, "Are we doing

209
00:10:45,722 --> 00:10:48,532
this now or later?" Never, "Are we doing

210
00:10:48,602 --> 00:10:51,602
this at all?" is running stage three, whether

211
00:10:51,662 --> 00:10:53,222
or not they learned it from a police

212
00:10:53,282 --> 00:10:56,482
manual. Sales environments run a gentler version of

213
00:10:56,542 --> 00:11:00,002
the same architecture constantly. Presumption becomes,

214
00:11:00,222 --> 00:11:03,252
"So when you get started with this," instead

215
00:11:03,302 --> 00:11:06,762
of if. Minimization becomes, "Most people

216
00:11:06,822 --> 00:11:09,192
in your position would agree this is a

217
00:11:09,261 --> 00:11:12,542
no-brainer," lowering the psychological cost of

218
00:11:12,602 --> 00:11:15,602
saying yes to something you haven't actually

219
00:11:15,642 --> 00:11:18,762
decided on yet. And the false alternative

220
00:11:18,842 --> 00:11:21,482
becomes the oldest trick in the closing playbook.

221
00:11:22,022 --> 00:11:24,242
"Would you prefer the monthly plan or the

222
00:11:24,402 --> 00:11:27,592
annual plan?" A question that has already quietly

223
00:11:27,662 --> 00:11:30,642
deleted the option of choosing neither. None of

224
00:11:30,682 --> 00:11:33,322
this requires a locked room or a uniform.

225
00:11:34,122 --> 00:11:36,562
It only requires you not noticing that a

226
00:11:36,582 --> 00:11:39,402
decision got made for you before you realized

227
00:11:39,442 --> 00:11:42,522
one was being asked. The defense against all

228
00:11:42,542 --> 00:11:45,942
three stages is the same single move. Go

229
00:11:46,062 --> 00:11:49,462
back to the assumption nobody let you question.

230
00:11:49,542 --> 00:11:51,522
Not which of these two things is true,

231
00:11:52,142 --> 00:11:54,862
but wait, is either of these actually true

232
00:11:54,922 --> 00:11:58,422
at all? Reclaiming the option that was quietly

233
00:11:58,442 --> 00:12:01,162
removed from the table is usually enough to

234
00:12:01,282 --> 00:12:04,742
break the entire structure because the whole technique

235
00:12:04,782 --> 00:12:07,442
depends on you never noticing it was taken

236
00:12:07,502 --> 00:12:10,092
away in the first place. Watch for the

237
00:12:10,202 --> 00:12:13,362
specific language too. Minimization

238
00:12:13,422 --> 00:12:16,772
often sounds like generosity. "I'm not judging you.

239
00:12:17,422 --> 00:12:20,022
I just need to understand," right before a

240
00:12:20,042 --> 00:12:23,182
demand that only makes sense if you've already

241
00:12:23,192 --> 00:12:26,322
agreed to the premise underneath it. And false

242
00:12:26,402 --> 00:12:28,952
alternatives almost always come as a pair of

243
00:12:29,042 --> 00:12:32,122
options offered a little too quickly, a little

244
00:12:32,222 --> 00:12:34,802
too smoothly, as if the real menu had

245
00:12:34,862 --> 00:12:37,222
already been narrowed down for you before you

246
00:12:37,262 --> 00:12:39,538
sat at the table. The moment you feel

247
00:12:39,618 --> 00:12:42,758
yourself choosing between two paths without remembering

248
00:12:42,819 --> 00:12:45,078
deciding you were on either one, that's the

249
00:12:45,139 --> 00:12:47,338
signal to stop and ask who built this

250
00:12:47,418 --> 00:12:50,358
particular fork in the road and why. There’s

251
00:12:50,418 --> 00:12:52,858
a slower version of this worth watching for

252
00:12:52,958 --> 00:12:56,378
too, one that plays out over weeks instead

253
00:12:56,418 --> 00:12:59,639
of minutes: a business partner who spends a

254
00:12:59,718 --> 00:13:02,698
month establishing “we both know this deal is

255
00:13:02,778 --> 00:13:06,139
happening” before ever discussing terms, so

256
00:13:06,179 --> 00:13:08,179
that by the time the actual numbers come

257
00:13:08,258 --> 00:13:11,038
up, backing out feels like betraying an

258
00:13:11,139 --> 00:13:13,538
agreement you don’t remember fully making.

259
00:13:14,398 --> 00:13:17,358
Presumption doesn’t need a locked room to work.

260
00:13:17,999 --> 00:13:20,499
It just needs enough repetition, calmly

261
00:13:20,558 --> 00:13:23,938
delivered, that the assumed conclusion starts

